00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:01.259
Hello!

00:00:01.569 --> 00:00:05.010
Welcome to this video in which we'll
look at the different types of waste

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.890
associated with lean management.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.470
You will often hear about the seven wastes of
lean, which are common amongst practitioners.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.860
These are 1.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.810
Transport, 2.

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:20.750
Inventory, 3.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.519
Motion, 4.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.420
Weighting, 5.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.720
Overproduction, 6.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:29.740
Overprocessing.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:32.990
7.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:34.290
Defects.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.210
In this video, we are also going
to look at the eighth waste.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:44.730
Unutilised talent is specifically related to
manufacturing management, whereas the first

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:47.699
seven are related to manufacturing processes.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.120
Let's look at each of these wastes in more detail.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.940
Transport waste occurs when movement of any
material isn't part of the immediate production.

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:05.224
Unnecessary movement of materials,
products or people which can lead to

00:01:05.244 --> 00:01:08.235
increased costs and risk of damage or loss.

00:01:08.804 --> 00:01:13.455
For example, transporting raw
materials or finished products between

00:01:13.455 --> 00:01:18.484
sites over long distances or having
multiple handoffs between departments.

00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:21.085
Inventory.

00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:25.355
Too much stock leads to more
than just transport waste.

00:01:25.895 --> 00:01:29.345
Lean processing is all about
just in time manufacturing.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.619
If production isn't lean, raw materials may be
over ordered, meaning they need to be stored.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:42.090
Cash flow is affected as money is being spent on
stock that might not be needed for months to come.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:48.919
We can see here that inventory waste
can be a result of inefficient processes

00:01:49.269 --> 00:01:51.509
and unbalanced production processes.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:53.840
Motion.

00:01:54.625 --> 00:02:00.414
Any job that requires excess movement,
such as reaching, bending, gathering

00:02:00.414 --> 00:02:02.905
tools or moving equipment is a waste.

00:02:03.435 --> 00:02:08.854
Motion waste is usually caused by ineffective
layout and poor workplace organisation.

00:02:09.405 --> 00:02:13.874
We've seen in Unit 2 that there are many
factors to consider when setting up an

00:02:13.874 --> 00:02:16.384
activity workplace and motion is another.

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:24.435
Lack of visual controls, poor processes, and
documentation will also lead to motion waste.

00:02:26.285 --> 00:02:26.795
Waiting.

00:02:27.565 --> 00:02:30.594
Any waiting time in the
production process is waste.

00:02:31.165 --> 00:02:36.015
For example, waiting for approval,
waiting for equipment to be repaired,

00:02:36.575 --> 00:02:38.734
or waiting for materials to arrive.

00:02:39.304 --> 00:02:44.614
Whilst these might not always be unavoidable,
they can be greatly reduced through strong

00:02:44.614 --> 00:02:51.915
processes, maintenance of equipment, and
consistent working methods over production.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:58.120
As with inventory waste, overproduction
procedures have similar waste, although

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.890
this happens for different reasons.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.609
When items are produced faster than needed,
it's commonly known as a push system.

00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:10.189
It can be caused by poor planning or
teams working at different speeds.

00:03:10.749 --> 00:03:15.979
Maybe there's new workers in one team, which
has slowed them down and lack of communication.

00:03:16.724 --> 00:03:21.505
Alternatively, the overproduction may be
down to incorrectly placed automation.

00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:24.295
Overprocessing.

00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:29.894
It's important to ensure that processing is
right for the job in hand, and that nothing

00:03:29.894 --> 00:03:32.114
additional to what's needed is being done.

00:03:32.674 --> 00:03:36.444
This could include producing items
that are above the quality needed,

00:03:36.924 --> 00:03:41.864
writing too many or too complicated
processes, and over refining processes.

00:03:42.595 --> 00:03:47.785
In summary, any unnecessary work that
results in more time and effort than is

00:03:47.785 --> 00:03:49.954
needed is considered over processing.

00:03:51.825 --> 00:03:52.524
Defects.

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:58.790
A good quality control process should minimise
defects, and it's therefore important to have

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.750
checks in place throughout the production.

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:05.689
Defects detected early in the production
can stop difficulties occurring for

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:11.510
teams further along, which can impact on
overproduction, waiting, and inventory.

00:04:12.039 --> 00:04:17.769
Where products are sent out with defects,
customers will return them, which leads to further

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:20.729
storage and time spent on customer complaints.

00:04:22.609 --> 00:04:23.955
Unutilised talent.

00:04:24.725 --> 00:04:29.095
All of the wastes we've looked at so
far relate to manufacturing processes.

00:04:29.695 --> 00:04:34.164
As mentioned at the beginning of
this video, unutilised talent is the

00:04:34.175 --> 00:04:36.234
only waste relating to management.

00:04:36.795 --> 00:04:42.015
It's a result of employees not being
engaged in processes, talent being wasted,

00:04:42.034 --> 00:04:44.575
or work being performed unnecessarily.

00:04:45.115 --> 00:04:49.435
Knowing and utilising employees
strengths results in better productivity.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:54.270
It's also important that employees are
encouraged to share knowledge, and we

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.620
considered the benefits of this in Unit 1.

